            <!--The difference between /js/script.js and ../../public/js/script.js lies in the way the path to the file is specified:

/js/script.js: This is an absolute path. The leading / tells the browser to look for the file starting from the root directory of the server. 
So, it will look for the script.js file inside a js directory located at the root of your server.
../../public/js/script.js: This is a relative path. The ../ tells the browser to go up one level. So, if you have two ../, it tells the browser to go up two levels. 
Then it will look for the script.js file inside a js directory, 
which is inside a public directory, which is two levels up from the current directory.
The reason one might work over the other depends on the structure of your project and where the HTML file referencing the JavaScript file is located. 
If your HTML file is not located in the root directory, /js/script.js might not work. Similarly,
 if your HTML file is not located in a directory two levels down from public, ../../public/js/script.js might not work. 
 It’s all about the relative positions of your files in your project’s directory structure. -->
            <script src="../../public/js/script.js"></script>
